    "evidence_text": "Our analysis sheds light on the shortcomings of current state-of-the-art models, and shows that non-expert annotators are successful at finding their weaknesses.\n\nWith the rapid pace of advancement in AI, however, NLU benchmarks struggle to keep up with model improvement.\n\nstronger model.\n\nWe then subject the strengthened model to human interference and collect more weaknesses over several rounds.\n\nHere, we focus on natural language inference (NLI), arguably the most canonical task in NLU.\n\nWe collected three rounds of data, and call our new dataset Adversarial NLI (ANLI).\n\n2) We show that training models on this new dataset leads to\n\nstate-of-the-art performance on a variety of popular NLI benchmarks.\n\nThe ANLI dataset is available at github.com/facebookresearch/anli/.\n\nA demo of the annotation procedure can be viewed at adversarialnli.com.\n\nDataset collection\tThe primary aim of this work is to create a new large-scale NLI benchmark on which current state-of-the-art models fail.\n\nThis constitutes a new target for the field to work towards, and can elucidate model capabilities and limitations.\n\nAs noted, however, static benchmarks do not last very long these days.\n\nIn our setup, our starting point is a base model, trained on NLI data.\n\nRather\n\ndesign.\n\nResults ::: Rounds become increasingly more difficult.\n\nThis is true for all model architectures.\n\nResults ::: Training on more rounds improves robustness.\n\nSimply training on more \u201cnormal NLI\u201d data would not help a model be robust to adversarial attacks, but our data actively helps mitigate these.\n\nResults ::: RoBERTa achieves state-of-the-art performance...\n\nand MNLI, but not vice versa.\n\nResults ::: Exclusive test subset difference is small.\n\nTable TABREF21 reports results on the three rounds of ANLI, as well as SNLI and MNLI.\n\nThe table shows some interesting take-aways:\n\nResults ::: Hypothesis-only results ::: Hypothesis-only models perform poorly on ANLI.\n\nWe corroborate that hypothesis-only models obtain good performance on\n\nSNLI and MNLI.\n\nPerformance of such models on ANLI is substantially lower, and decreases with more rounds.\n\nThis could mean two things: either the test data is very difficult, or the training data is not good.\n\nAnalysis\tWe perform two types of model error analysis.\n\n(e.g., SNLI and MNLI).\n\nWe observe that all of our models far outperform the models\n\npresented in original papers for these common stress tests, with our two RoBERTa models performing best.\n\nTraining a RoBERTa model also on ANLI appears to be particularly useful for the NR, WO, NG and AT NLI stress tests.\n\nAnalysis ::: Reasoning types\tA dynamically evolving dataset offers the unique opportunity to track how model error rates change over time.\n\nWe employed an expert linguist annotator to devise an ontology of inference types that would be specific to NLI.\n\nused to improve image captioning systems .\n\nOur dataset is not to be confused with abductive NLI , which calls itself $\\alpha $NLI, or ART.\n\nThe benchmark is designed to be challenging to current state of the art models.\n\nWe found that non-expert annotators, in this gamified setting and with appropriate incentives to fool the model, are remarkably creative at finding and exploiting weaknesses in models.\n\nTraining on this new data\n\nyielded the state of the art on existing NLI benchmarks.\n\nThe ANLI benchmark presents a new challenge to the community.\n\nThe dataset also presents many opportunities for further study.\n\nFor instance, we collected annotator-provided explanations for each example that the model got wrong.\n\nWe provided inference labels for the development set, opening up possibilities for interesting more fine-grained studies of NLI model performance.\n\nAs a first next step, it would be interesting to examine results when annotators are confronted with a wide variety of model architectures.\n\nLuckily, if it does turn out to",
